---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const github='https://github.com/MMS-21/hr-attrition-analysis';
const python=`def classify_experience(years):
    if years < 5:
        return "Junior"
    elif years <= 9:
        return "Mid"
    return "Senior"

df["ExperienceLevel"] = df["TotalWorkingYears"].apply(
    classify_experience
)`;
const bridge=`sheets = pd.read_excel("prepared_data.xlsx", sheet_name=None)

con = duckdb.connect()
con.register("DimDepartment", sheets["DimDepartment"])
con.register("DimJobRole", sheets["DimJobRole"])
con.register("FactEmployee", sheets["FactEmployee"])

result = con.execute(query).fetchdf()`;
const sql=`SELECT
    d.Department,
    j.JobRoleName AS JobRole,
    COUNT(*) AS EmployeeCount,
    COUNT(CASE WHEN f.AttritionFlag = 1 THEN 1 END)
        AS AttritionCount,
    100.0 * COUNT(CASE WHEN f.AttritionFlag = 1 THEN 1 END)
        / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0) AS AttritionRatePct,
    AVG(f.MonthlyIncome) AS AvgMonthlyIncome
FROM FactEmployee f
INNER JOIN DimDepartment d
    ON f.DepartmentKey = d.DepartmentKey
INNER JOIN DimJobRole j
    ON f.JobRoleKey = j.JobRoleKey
GROUP BY d.Department, j.JobRoleName
ORDER BY AttritionCount DESC;`;
---
<Base title="Employee Attrition Analysis" description="Exploring employee attrition across roles, overtime, and experience, and turning the findings into a practical retention briefing." hideFooter>
 <article class="hr-case">
  <a class="back" href="/projects/">← All projects</a>
  <header class="case-opening">
   <h1>Where should HR focus its retention efforts?</h1>
   <p class="lead">Exploring employee attrition across roles, overtime, and experience, and turning the findings into a practical retention briefing.</p>
   <div class="case-meta"><span>Power BI · DAX · SQL · Python · DuckDB</span><span>Confidential</span></div>
   <a class="source-link" href={github}>Explore the code on GitHub ↗</a>
  </header>
  <figure class="opening-chart"><a href="/img/hr/attrition-overview.svg" aria-label="Open attrition comparison chart at full size"><img src="/img/hr/attrition-overview.svg" width="960" height="580" alt="Recorded attrition: overall 16.1%, overtime 30.5%, no overtime 10.4%, Sales Representatives 39.8%, Laboratory Technicians 23.9%." /></a><figcaption>Checked analysis preview. Each percentage uses its own group as the denominator.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="story-row"><h2>The average hides the question.</h2><div><p>Across 1,470 employee records, 237 were marked as leavers, an overall proportion of 16.1%. That average is useful context, but it does not tell HR which groups deserve a closer look.</p><p>I explored differences across roles, overtime, and total working experience, then brought the findings into a reporting workflow and a practical retention briefing.</p></div></section>
  <section class="story-row"><h2>From records to reporting.</h2><div><p>My work covered data preparation, a Power BI dashboard, DAX measures, SQL analysis, Python segmentation, and stakeholder communication.</p><ol><li>Prepare employee records and department, job role, and employee dimensions.</li><li>Build reporting measures for employee counts, attrition proportions, income comparisons, and overtime.</li><li>Use Python and SQL together to inspect group differences and check the results.</li><li>Translate those comparisons into questions HR can investigate and proposed next steps.</li></ol></div></section>
  <section class="findings"><h2>Three patterns worth investigating</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Selected employee groups in the analysed sample</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">Recorded leavers / group</th><th scope="col">Attrition proportion</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Overtime</th><td>127 / 416</td><td>30.5%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">No overtime</th><td>110 / 1,054</td><td>10.4%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sales Representatives</th><td>33 / 83</td><td>39.8%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Under 5 years of total experience</th><td>75 / 228</td><td>32.9%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These differences suggest questions about workload, role progression, compensation, and support for less experienced employees. They do not establish why any individual left.</p><p class="note">Experience means total working years, not time at this employer. These are proportions in the supplied sample, with no confirmed annual observation window.</p></section>
  <section class="code-section"><h2>Python made the groups useful.</h2><p>I classified total working years into Junior, Mid, and Senior groups, then summarised employee counts. The thresholds were under 5, 5 through 9, and 10 or more years.</p><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Python experience classification"><code>{python}</code></pre><p>The groups contain 228, 493, and 749 records respectively. Their recorded leaver counts are 75, 82, and 80. The public script adds validation for missing columns, employee uniqueness, flags, and dimension keys.</p></section>
  <section class="code-section"><h2>Python and SQL, in one workflow.</h2><p>I used pandas to load the prepared tables, registered the DataFrames with an in-memory DuckDB connection, and brought SQL results back into pandas with <code>fetchdf()</code>.</p><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Python DuckDB integration"><code>{bridge}</code></pre><p>This let me query the prepared data without setting up a separate database server. The workflow reads and analyses data; it does not modify a persistent production database.</p><h3>Joining the business context</h3><p>The query connects employee records to department and role names, then compares leaver counts, group sizes, attrition proportions, and average income. This public version extends my original aggregation with counts and proportions.</p><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="SQL attrition aggregation"><code>{sql}</code></pre><a href={`${github}/blob/main/src/analyse.py`}>Read the runnable workflow ↗</a></section>
  <section class="story-row"><h2>Power BI brought it together.</h2><div><p>The report includes a Landing Page, Executive Overview, Deep Dive, and Recommendations Preview. It uses prepared employee and dimension tables with DAX measures for the selected reporting context.</p><p>Measures cover employee counts, attrition proportion, retained versus departed income, and the overtime segment. The public repository documents the model and measures and includes report screenshots. The original report file remains withheld.</p><a href={`${github}/tree/main/powerbi`}>Inspect the model notes and DAX ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="report-gallery" aria-labelledby="report-pages"><h2 id="report-pages">Inside the Power BI report</h2><p>Four captures from my original report. Open an image to inspect it at full size. The views show the report as captured, including its original labels and navigation.</p>
   <figure><a href="/img/hr/executive-overview.png" aria-label="Open Power BI Executive Overview"><img src="/img/hr/executive-overview.png" width="1224" height="688" loading="lazy" alt="Power BI employee metrics, decomposition tree, role counts, and global filters." /></a><figcaption>Executive Overview. The role chart shows leaver counts, while the decomposition tree shows group attrition proportions. Its drill-down is descriptive and does not establish root causes.</figcaption></figure>
   <figure><a href="/img/hr/deep-dive.png" aria-label="Open Power BI Deep Dive"><img src="/img/hr/deep-dive.png" width="1224" height="688" loading="lazy" alt="Power BI age comparison, income and working-years scatter plot, income-band counts, and experience by overtime matrix." /></a><figcaption>Deep Dive. The income-band stacked chart shows employee counts despite its original percentage heading. The experience matrix compares proportions within each experience and overtime group.</figcaption></figure>
   <details><summary>See the landing page and recommendation scenario</summary>
    <figure><a href="/img/hr/landing-page.png" aria-label="Open Power BI landing page"><img src="/img/hr/landing-page.png" width="1274" height="696" loading="lazy" alt="Original Power BI landing page with report navigation and purpose." /></a><figcaption>Landing Page. The original navigation and report introduction.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><a href="/img/hr/recommendations.png" aria-label="Open Power BI recommendations scenario"><img src="/img/hr/recommendations.png" width="1224" height="688" loading="lazy" alt="Power BI what-if scenario, replacement-cost assumptions, and proposed action plan." /></a><figcaption>Recommendations Preview. The savings figure is a hypothetical scenario based on the displayed assumptions. It is not a measured saving or a validated forecast.</figcaption></figure>
   </details>
  </section>
  <section class="story-row"><h2>What I would investigate next.</h2><div><p>Start with workload reviews in overtime-heavy teams, examine compensation and progression for Sales Representatives, and explore onboarding and mentoring needs among less experienced employees.</p><p>Validate these explanations with further evidence before choosing an intervention. Follow-up reporting should assess whether a tested action improves retention.</p><p class="note">This project demonstrates reporting and descriptive analysis. It does not claim a validated prediction model, measured retention improvement, or savings delivered.</p></div></section>
  <footer class="case-close"><h2>See how the analysis fits together.</h2><p>The repository includes cleaned Python, SQL, DAX, model notes, and checked charts.</p><a href={github}>View the project on GitHub ↗</a><a href="/projects/">Explore more projects →</a></footer>
 </article>
</Base>
